    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Historians widely agree that preserving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was the principal aim of secession, as the states' own secession documents show; in 1860 enslaved people were 39 per cent of the eleven states' population and 31 per cent of free households owned slaves.</li><li>Its permanent constitution copied much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> verbatim but added explicit protections for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in every territory, forbade any state to bar slaveholders from bringing their slaves in, and kept the ban on the African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</li><li>Outnumbered about four to one in military manpower and far more in industry and railroads, the Confederacy passed the first national conscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> on 16 April 1862, financed itself by printing money until flour cost $100 a barrel in Richmond, and won recognition from no foreign government.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>No foreign government ever recognised the Confederacy: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> Dudley Mann reported a letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> addressed to &quot;the Illustrious and Honorable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a>, President of the Confederate States of America&quot;, Secretary of State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> P. Benjamin replied that Mann had mistranslated it and that it was &quot;a mere inferential recognition&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

